1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,763
Well, I've been talking to managers in a number of businesses,

2
00:00:03,763 --> 00:00:06,500
and reading surveys about the future of management.

3
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:15,330
And what I'm going to present in this seminar is a few ideas about how the activity is likely to change in the next ten years.

4
00:00:15,330 --> 00:00:21,610
It isn't a scientific, statistical analysis - just some ideas for us to discuss.

5
00:00:21,610 --> 00:00:25,310
One area I want to mention is business markets,

6
00:00:25,310 --> 00:00:31,066
and I'm sure a really significant development will be a major increase in competition,

7
00:00:31,066 --> 00:00:36,000
with companies from all round the world trying to sell similar products.

8
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:39,938
Consumers will have much more choice - for instance,

9
00:00:39,938 --> 00:00:44,860
food products sold in Australia might be manufactured in the USA

10
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:48,800
China, Finland and dozens of other countries.

11
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:51,203
At the same time, mergers

12
00:00:51,203 --> 00:00:57,450
and takeovers mean that governments are actually losing power to major global corporations.

13
00:00:57,450 --> 00:01:02,980
We can probably all think of companies that exert a great deal of influence

14
00:01:03,320 --> 00:01:05,340
which may be good for consumers.

15
00:01:05,340 --> 00:01:12,637
A third point I want to make about markets is that in the rapidly expanding economies,

16
00:01:12,637 --> 00:01:18,110
such as India, China, Brazil and Russia, demand is growing very fast.

17
00:01:18,110 --> 00:01:22,420
This is putting pressure on resources all over the world.

18
00:01:22,420 --> 00:01:27,930
I think businesses are becoming more open to external influences.

19
00:01:27,930 --> 00:01:31,960
In particular, companies are consulting customers more

20
00:01:31,960 --> 00:01:34,680
and more before making their business decisions.

21
00:01:34,680 --> 00:01:37,984
Companies are finding out what they want and providing it,

22
00:01:37,984 --> 00:01:41,540
instead of making products and then trying to sell them

23
00:01:41,780 --> 00:01:43,600
which is the model of years ago.

24
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:50,689
Another influence is that concerns about the environment will force manufacturers to extend product lifecycles,

25
00:01:50,689 --> 00:01:54,470
to reduce the amount of pollution and waste.

26
00:01:54,470 --> 00:01:59,850
And in some cases. regulation will need to be strengthened.

27
00:01:59,850 --> 00:02:04,680
Many societies are much more fluid and democratic

28
00:02:04,940 --> 00:02:08,000
and the structure of companies is changing to reflect that.

29
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:13,565
I think we're going to see a greater emphasis within companies on teams created

30
00:02:13,565 --> 00:02:15,950
with a specific project in mind.

31
00:02:15,950 --> 00:02:21,300
And when they're completed, the teams will be disbanded and new ones formed.

32
00:02:21,300 --> 00:02:26,141
More and more people see work as simply one part of their lifestyle,

33
00:02:26,141 --> 00:02:31,727
and not the most important one, and as the workforce is shrinking in some countries,

34
00:02:31,727 --> 00:02:38,430
businesses are having to compete for staff instead of being able to choose among a lot of applicants.

35
00:02:38,430 --> 00:02:41,400
Typical examples that will attract

36
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:45,507
and retain staff are traditional ones like flexible hours and -

37
00:02:45,507 --> 00:02:50,225
something that has been made possible by advances in technology -

38
00:02:50,225 --> 00:02:53,560
remote working, with people based at their home

39
00:02:53,660 --> 00:02:56,830
abroad, or almost anywhere they choose.

40
00:02:56,830 --> 00:03:05,370
Management styles will almost certainly continue to change.

41
00:03:05,370 --> 00:03:10,720
Senior managers will require a lot more than the efficiency that they've always needed.

42
00:03:10,720 --> 00:03:14,520
Above all they'll need great skills in leadership,

43
00:03:14,520 --> 00:03:17,640
so that their organisation can initiate

44
00:03:17,640 --> 00:03:21,170
and respond to change in a fast-moving world,

45
00:03:21,170 --> 00:03:25,920
where they face lots of competing requirements and potential conflicts.

46
00:03:25,920 --> 00:03:28,200
In most of the world

47
00:03:28,360 --> 00:03:33,440
the senior managers of large businesses are mainly men in their fifties and sixties.

48
00:03:33,440 --> 00:03:39,886
The predominant style of management will almost certainly become more consultative and collaborative,

49
00:03:39,886 --> 00:03:45,340
caused above all, by more women moving into senior management positions.

50
00:03:45,340 --> 00:03:50,630
Many of the changes are influenced by developments in the wider economy.

51
00:03:50,630 --> 00:03:54,740
The traditional emphasis of business was manufacturing

52
00:03:54,840 --> 00:03:58,050
and of course the service sector is very important.

53
00:03:58,050 --> 00:04:05,800
But we shouldn't overlook the growing financial contribution of IP, that is, intellectual property.

54
00:04:05,800 --> 00:04:12,300
Some books and films generate enormous sums from the sale of related DVDs

55
00:04:12,460 --> 00:04:15,870
music, games, clothes, and so on.

56
00:04:15,870 --> 00:04:20,959
Another point I'd like to make is that although I've been talking about companies,

57
00:04:20,959 --> 00:04:28,230
one trend that they have to face is the move away from people working for the same employer for years.

58
00:04:28,230 --> 00:04:31,696
Instead, more and more people are becoming self-employed,

59
00:04:31,696 --> 00:04:39,060
to gain the freedom and control over their lives that they're unlikely to get from being employed.

60
00:04:39,060 --> 00:04:44,920
OK, well that's all I want to say, so let's open it up for discussion.
